---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { BRIDGE_IMAGE, SELFHOST_RAW_URL, SOURCE_REPO_URL, SUPPORT_EMAIL, UPDATED } from "../config";
import { path } from "../i18n/ui";

const description =
  "Run your own Garage Opener bridge: Protect API key, relay and sensor setup, network requirements, Docker, first-run pairing, Alarm Manager webhooks, push alerts and reaching it from outside the house.";

const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "HowTo",
  name: "Run your own Garage Opener bridge",
  inLanguage: "en-US",
  description,
  step: [
    { "@type": "HowToStep", name: "Prepare the hardware in UniFi Protect", text: "Set one relay output to Pulse and mount the all-in-one sensor on the door with mount type Garage." },
    { "@type": "HowToStep", name: "Create a Protect API key", text: "Console settings, Control Plane, Integrations, Create API Key." },
    { "@type": "HowToStep", name: "Check the network paths", text: "The bridge must reach the console on 443, and the console and your phone must reach the bridge on 8787." },
    { "@type": "HowToStep", name: "Run the bridge in Docker", text: "Download the reference docker-compose.yml and .env, fill in the key and generated secrets, and start it." },
    { "@type": "HowToStep", name: "Pair your phone", text: "Paste the single-use invite link the bridge prints at startup into the app." },
    { "@type": "HowToStep", name: "Point Alarm Manager at the bridge", text: "Add sensor opened and closed rules posting to the webhook URL." },
  ],
};

const raw = SELFHOST_RAW_URL;
---

<Base
  locale="en"
  route="selfHosting"
  title="Self-hosting the bridge"
  description={description}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  updated={UPDATED}
  toc={[
    { id: "before", label: "Before you start" },
    { id: "network", label: "What has to reach what" },
    { id: "run", label: "Run it" },
    { id: "pair", label: "Pair your phone" },
    { id: "webhooks", label: "Console webhooks" },
    { id: "alerts", label: "Alerts on a sleeping phone" },
    { id: "certs", label: "Certificates" },
    { id: "remote", label: "From outside the house" },
    { id: "updating", label: "Updates and backups" },
    { id: "source", label: "Source and licence" },
    { id: "scope", label: "Out of scope" },
  ]}
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>Self-hosting the bridge</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      The bridge is one small service in a container. It talks to your Protect console, keeps the door state
      machine, serves the app and runs the alert rules. It runs on a NAS, a Raspberry Pi or any box with
      Docker, on amd64 and arm64.
    </p>

    <div class="note">
      <p>
        <strong>You don't have to run it.</strong> The app connects straight to your Protect console and works
        on your home network with nothing else installed. Run the bridge if you want always-on alert rules, an
        exportable activity log, family invites, or background-reliable Shortcuts.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="before">Before you start</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Set one relay output to Pulse.</strong> In Protect, open the relay and configure the output
        that is wired to your opener's push-button terminals as <em>Pulse</em>, type garage door. Note which
        output it is: the numbering in the app starts at 1, but the API's own ids start at 0.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Mount the sensor and set its mount type to Garage.</strong> This is what the bridge reads to
        decide whether the door is open. If the mount type is anything else, auto-discovery will not pair it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Create a Protect API key.</strong> Console → Settings → Control Plane → Integrations → Create
        API Key. Copy it once; it is not shown again.
        <div class="note">
          <p>
            This key can read every camera on your console. Treat it like a password to your house, because
            functionally that is what it is.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      <strong>How the bridge finds your door.</strong> With exactly one relay output of type garage door and
      one sensor mounted as Garage, the bridge finds both on its own and you configure nothing. With more than
      one candidate it cannot guess, so pin them: <code>DOOR_RELAY_ID</code>, <code>DOOR_OUTPUT_ID</code> (the
      API id, so output 1 in the app is <code>0</code>) and <code>DOOR_SENSOR_ID</code>. Once the bridge is
      running, it lists every relay and sensor with its id:
    </p>
    <pre><code>{`curl -s -H "Authorization: Bearer <BRIDGE_TOKENS>" http://<bridge-host>:8787/v1/discovery`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      <strong>Relay press.</strong> On Protect 7.2.x the relay toggles instead of pulsing, so the bridge
      presses and releases it itself. That is the default, <code>RELAY_PULSE_MODE=emulated</code>. Only if
      your relay genuinely pulses on its own, set <code>RELAY_PULSE_MODE=native</code>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="network">What has to reach what</h2>
    <p>
      Everything stays on your network. If the bridge and the console sit on different VLANs, your firewall
      has to allow both of the first two rows, in the direction shown.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>From</th><th>To</th><th>Port</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Bridge</td><td>Protect console</td><td>TCP 443 (HTTPS)</td><td>Reading the sensor, pressing the relay. Required.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Protect console</td><td>Bridge</td><td>TCP 8787 (HTTP)</td><td>Alarm Manager webhooks, so the bridge reacts instantly. Recommended; it polls anyway.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Phone</td><td>Bridge</td><td>TCP 8787 (HTTP)</td><td>The app, widgets, Watch and Shortcuts. Required: on your Wi-Fi, or over a VPN or tunnel when away.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Phone</td><td>Bridge</td><td>UDP 5353 (mDNS)</td><td>Finding the bridge without typing its address. Optional; needs host networking and does not cross VLANs unless your gateway reflects mDNS.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Bridge</td><td><code>ghcr.io</code></td><td>TCP 443</td><td>Downloading the image, when you install or update.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The phone must be on a network that can reach the bridge, with client isolation off on that Wi-Fi.
      Never forward a port from the internet to the bridge; see <a href="#remote">from outside the house</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="run">Run it</h2>
    <p>
      The image is <code>{BRIDGE_IMAGE}</code>. The reference <a href={`${SOURCE_REPO_URL}/blob/main/selfhost/docker-compose.yml`}>docker-compose.yml</a>
      and <a href={`${SOURCE_REPO_URL}/blob/main/selfhost/.env.example`}>.env.example</a> live in the bridge
      repository. Put both in one directory:
    </p>
    <pre><code>{`mkdir garage-bridge && cd garage-bridge
curl -fsSLO ${raw}/docker-compose.yml
curl -fsSL  ${raw}/.env.example -o .env`}</code></pre>

    <p>
      Open <code>.env</code> and fill in the values at the top. Two of them are secrets you generate, not
      invent. Run this twice and paste each output into place:
    </p>
    <pre><code>openssl rand -hex 24</code></pre>
    <pre><code>{`# .env: the values that matter
GARAGE_BRIDGE_TAG=latest     # see "updates and backups" below
PROTECT_URL=https://192.168.1.1
PROTECT_API_KEY=…            # the key you just created
BRIDGE_TOKENS=…              # first openssl output: your admin token
WEBHOOK_SECRET=…             # second openssl output: the Alarm Manager path secret
TZ=Europe/Zurich`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      <code>.env</code> is read as plain values, not run as a shell script, so a line like
      <code>BRIDGE_TOKENS=$(openssl rand -hex 24)</code> would store those characters literally. Paste the
      output, not the command.
    </p>

    <pre><code>{`docker compose up -d
curl -s localhost:8787/healthz`}</code></pre>

    <p>
      You should get <code>{`{"ok":true,"ready":true,…}`}</code>, with the <code>version</code> that is
      actually running. If <code>ready</code> is <code>false</code>, the bridge is up but hasn't reached the
      console yet, and the message says why. Run the same <code>curl</code> from another machine, with the
      bridge host's address instead of <code>localhost</code>, to check the network path too.
    </p>

    <h3>Using Dockhand, Portainer or another stack manager</h3>
    <p>
      Paste the reference <code>docker-compose.yml</code> as the stack and set the same variables in the
      stack's environment instead of a <code>.env</code> file. The file lists every setting explicitly, so
      nothing else is needed. A variable that is not in the file never reaches the bridge.
    </p>

    <h3>A note on tokens</h3>
    <p>
      <code>BRIDGE_TOKENS</code> is the credential that opens your garage door. The bridge refuses to start on
      anything shorter than 24 characters, or on anything guessable. Generate it. Your family's phones do
      <em>not</em> need an entry here. They join by invite, below.
    </p>

    <h3>Host networking, or not</h3>
    <p>
      The app finds the bridge over Bonjour, and multicast does not cross Docker's bridge network, so the
      reference compose uses <code>network_mode: host</code>. If that isn't available to you, for instance on
      Docker Desktop for macOS or Windows, publish <code>8787:8787</code> instead, set
      <code>BONJOUR=false</code>, and set <code>PUBLIC_URL=http://&lt;host-address&gt;:8787</code>. Without
      host networking the bridge only sees its container's address, and that is what invite links would
      otherwise carry.
    </p>

    <h2 id="pair">Pair your phone</h2>
    <p>
      Don't copy your admin token into the app. While no phone has joined, the bridge prints a single-use
      invite at startup:
    </p>
    <pre><code>docker logs garage-bridge | tail -20</code></pre>
    <p>
      On the app's first screen, tap <strong>Join with an invite</strong> and paste the
      <code>garageopener://join…</code> link or just the code. Opening the link on the phone does the same.
      The invite carries the bridge's address: <code>PUBLIC_URL</code> if you
      set it, otherwise the host's LAN address. It's good for fifteen minutes; restart the container for a
      fresh one. Once a phone has joined, the banner stops and any further phones are invited from the app
      itself.
    </p>

    <h2 id="webhooks">Let the console tell the bridge about the door</h2>
    <p>
      The bridge polls, but webhooks make it react within a second. Create two rules on the console:
    </p>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Open Protect → <strong>Alarm Manager</strong> → <strong>Create Alarm</strong>.</li>
      <li>
        Name it <code>garage-door-opened</code>. Trigger: <strong>Sensor → Contact → Opened</strong>, on your
        garage door sensor.
      </li>
      <li>
        Under <strong>Actions</strong>, add <strong>Webhook</strong>, choose <strong>POST</strong>, untick
        <em>Send thumbnail</em>, and paste:
        <pre><code>http://&lt;bridge-host&gt;:8787/v1/webhooks/alarm-manager/&lt;WEBHOOK_SECRET&gt;</code></pre>
      </li>
      <li>Repeat with <code>garage-door-closed</code> and <strong>Contact → Closed</strong>. Keep the words <em>opened</em> and <em>closed</em> in the names.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      <strong>Check it worked</strong> by moving the door (or just the sensor). The app's activity log shows a
      <em>Sensor</em> entry within a second. Protect's <em>Test alarm</em> button sends a generic sample, not the real
      event, so it is not a useful test.
    </p>
    <p>
      If nothing arrives: a wrong secret returns a bare 404, so check the secret first, and that nothing
      trailed it when you pasted. Then check that the console can reach the bridge host (see
      <a href="#network">what has to reach what</a>).
    </p>

    <h2 id="alerts">Alerts on a sleeping phone</h2>
    <p>
      The bridge evaluates the alert rules around the clock. The app shows them whenever it is running or
      refreshing in the background, but iOS decides when that happens. For a notification that reliably
      reaches a phone that is asleep or away from home, have the bridge publish to
      <a href="https://ntfy.sh">ntfy</a> and subscribe to the topic in the ntfy app:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Your own ntfy</strong>: the reference compose includes one. Set <code>NTFY_BASE_URL</code> and
        <code>NTFY_TOPIC</code> in <code>.env</code>, then <code>docker compose --profile ntfy up -d</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>ntfy.sh</strong>: set <code>NTFY_URL=https://ntfy.sh</code> and a long random
        <code>NTFY_TOPIC</code> (<code>openssl rand -hex 16</code>). The topic name is the only thing keeping
        it private.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      With <code>PUBLIC_URL</code> set, ntfy notifications carry <em>Close now</em> and <em>Hold 2h</em> buttons
      that call your bridge directly.
    </p>

    <h2 id="certs">Certificates</h2>
    <p>
      Your console presents a self-signed certificate, so the default is <code>PROTECT_TLS=insecure</code>,
      which is fine on a LAN. Pin it properly once you're running:
    </p>
    <pre><code>{`openssl s_client -connect 192.168.1.1:443 -servername 192.168.1.1 </dev/null 2>/dev/null \\
  | openssl x509 -noout -fingerprint -sha256`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      Put the result in <code>PROTECT_TLS</code>. You can paste openssl's output as it is
      (<code>sha256 Fingerprint=AB:CD:…</code>); colons and case are ignored, and it is stored as
      <code>fingerprint:abcd…</code>. If your console has a real certificate, use <code>system</code>. The
      bridge <strong>refuses to start</strong> with <code>insecure</code> if <code>PROTECT_URL</code> is not a
      LAN address, because that would expose your API key to anyone on the path.
    </p>

    <h2 id="remote">Reaching it from outside the house</h2>
    <p>
      Set <code>PUBLIC_URL</code> to whatever address the phone will use, since that is what invite links carry. Then
      pick one:
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Approach</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>VPN back home</strong> (WireGuard on the UDM, or Tailscale)</td><td>Recommended. Nothing is exposed; the phone simply joins your network.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Cloudflare Tunnel</strong></td><td>Real hostname, real certificate, no inbound port. Set <code>TRUST_PROXY=true</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Your own reverse proxy</strong></td><td>Caddy, nginx or Traefik terminating TLS. Set <code>TRUST_PROXY=true</code>, and turn off response buffering on <code>/v1/events</code> or the door state will lag.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Port forwarding</strong></td><td>No. This puts a device that opens your garage on the public internet behind one token, usually without TLS.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <div class="note">
      <p>
        <code>TRUST_PROXY</code> matters more than it looks. Without it, every request appears to come from
        the proxy, so the per-address limit on invite claims becomes five a minute for your entire household.
        With it set when nothing in front strips <code>X-Forwarded-For</code>, a caller can forge its own
        address, so set it only when a proxy you control is genuinely there.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="updating">Updates and backups</h2>
    <p>
      <code>latest</code> is the default and is the right choice for most people. Docker never updates a
      container on its own, so <code>latest</code> simply means you get the current release the next time you
      choose to pull. That keeps you on security fixes without having to follow releases.
    </p>
    <pre><code>{`docker compose pull && docker compose up -d
curl -s localhost:8787/healthz   # check the version that came down`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      Pin an exact version instead, <code>GARAGE_BRIDGE_TAG=0.5.1</code>, if you want upgrades to be a
      decision rather than a side effect of pulling, or if you run something that pulls automatically such as
      Watchtower. With <code>latest</code> and an automatic updater together, a release that needs a
      configuration change would restart your bridge into a refusal to start while you are not looking.
    </p>
    <p>
      Either way, read the <a href={`${SOURCE_REPO_URL}/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md`}>changelog</a> before a
      pull. Anything that requires you to touch your configuration is listed under <strong>Action
      required</strong>, and the bridge fails loudly at startup rather than running in a degraded state.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Back up the <code>garage-bridge-data</code> volume.</strong> It holds the family members, open
      invites and the activity log. Lose it and every phone has to pair again. Your <code>.env</code> is the
      only other thing to keep, and it belongs in a password manager rather than a shared backup.
    </p>

    <h2 id="source">Source and licence</h2>
    <p>
      {SOURCE_REPO_URL
        ? <Fragment>The bridge and its OpenAPI contract are <a href={SOURCE_REPO_URL}>open source under Apache-2.0</a>. You are running it on your own hardware with a credential to your console, so you should be able to read it rather than take it on trust, including the <a href={`${SOURCE_REPO_URL}/blob/main/docs/security-review.md`}>security review</a> carried out before it was published.</Fragment>
        : <Fragment>The bridge is currently distributed as a container image (<code>{BRIDGE_IMAGE}</code>). Its source, and the OpenAPI contract with it, is being prepared for publication under Apache-2.0. This page will link to it when it is genuinely available, and not before.</Fragment>}
    </p>
    <p>
      The iOS app is not open source, and neither is the cloud-alerts service. That service is something we
      operate rather than something you run, it holds no credential to your console, and it cannot open or
      close anything. What it stores is set out in full on the <a href={path("en", "privacy")}>privacy page</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="scope">Out of scope</h2>
    <p>
      One door per bridge. UniFi Protect only, with no other relays and no other hubs. No Home Assistant integration.
      No Android. A non-Docker install (Node 22 directly) will run but is not supported: Docker is the tested
      path and the only one the instructions cover.
    </p>

    <p style="margin-top:2.5em">
      Stuck? <a href={path("en", "faq")}>The FAQ</a> covers the common ones, and
      <a href={`mailto:${SUPPORT_EMAIL}`}> {SUPPORT_EMAIL}</a> reaches a person.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
